[{:seon.task/name "durability-is-continuous-group-commit"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged): continuous group commit, with no window and no clock (DECISIONS: Durability is batched everywhere). `seon.writer/writer-proc` answers a batch once committed in memory and, when the store is dirty and no notice is outstanding, sends the flusher {:op :dirty :commits n}; `seon.writer/flusher-proc` syncs at once (`seon.db/sync!`), one sync covering every notice queued meanwhile, and answers {:op :flushed :commits n}; the writer is clean when n is still its commit count, else it notifies again. A :flush? request (one an outside effect follows) syncs before its answer. `seon.agent/transact!` commits at idle, at turn end and before any wait (`seon.agent/writes-commit-before-any-wait`), never because time passed. Replaced: the flusher's 10 ms sleep on ``seon.db/flush-ms`` and the 10 ms commit clock in `seon.agent/transact!` (both deleted). Not taken: syncing inline after each batch, because a Flow step's answers leave only after its transform returns, so every answer would wait for the fsync. v1 had no flusher: every recording was one synchronous transaction, durable before its reply (~/src/seon CLAUDE.md, \"Recover facts, not execution\"). Cost: per batch one notice; per sync one env sync and one answer; an idle system takes no step."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/the-flush-is-continuous-group-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "main-notices-carry-a-commit-not-a-db"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.writer/writer-proc` sends each main commit as a notice {:t :ops :db} (src/seon/writer.clj, the :main notice); the reconciler's :main is a blocking buffer and the vectors proc keeps :latest, so each queued commit pins its own db version, and a stale version pins the nodes later transactions rewrote (AGENTS section 4, a kept db value is a reference; docs/machine.md#roots-heap). The fork server already takes a commit and makes its db with d/commit-as-db when it takes it (`seon.fork-server/server-step` :tx). Correct: the notice carries {:t :ops :commit}; the reconciler, the render proc and the vectors proc make the db when they take the notice and let it go when the next replaces it. Replaces the :db in the notice and in the vectors proc's :latest; adds nothing. Not changed: the reconciler's :db and the render proc's :main-db, each replaced at every commit (research 2026-10-29: 17 lookups read it, re-creating it saves nothing). Check first that each consumer reaches the store (the conn) to call commit-as-db. Measure: retained bytes of the three procs with 200 commits queued, and ticks per commit through the cost proofs, before and after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.reconcile-test/an-index-is-observed-at-the-cost-of-what-changed seon.vector-cost-test/a-commit-is-embedded-for-what-it-changed seon.procs-test/the-writers-main-notice-holds-no-db}}]
